1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,818
Before generating results, we freeze the prompts, seeds, renderer, sampled frames, scoring method, and pass thresholds.

2
00:00:06,818 --> 00:00:12,662
This is called preregistration: deciding what counts as success before seeing which samples look good.

3
00:00:12,662 --> 00:00:16,329
We test three prompts, three seeds, and three prompt conditions.

4
00:00:16,329 --> 00:00:21,600
An automatic text-image scorer compares three frames from each video with the intended text.

5
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:28,735
The first test asks whether a generated video matches its intended prompt better than the other two prompt labels.

6
00:00:28,735 --> 00:00:36,637
The second asks whether correct text produces a better video than wrong or empty text, while keeping the intended meaning fixed for scoring.

7
00:00:36,637 --> 00:00:38,274
The first checks recognition.

8
00:00:38,274 --> 00:00:40,137
The second checks causal control.

9
00:00:40,137 --> 00:00:44,540
We also count individual wins so a good average cannot hide repeated failures.

10
00:00:45,240 --> 00:00:50,637
Across nine videos made by the exact recipe writer, eight were matched to the intended prompt.

11
00:00:50,637 --> 00:00:57,296
Correctly prompted videos beat wrong-prompt videos in all nine comparisons and beat empty-prompt videos in all nine.

12
00:00:57,296 --> 00:01:00,799
The average margins cleared the thresholds we set in advance.

13
00:01:00,799 --> 00:01:04,760
The field-size, rendering, and irregular-position checks also passed.

14
00:01:05,460 --> 00:01:08,267
The exact writer uses hand-built prompt lookup.

15
00:01:08,267 --> 00:01:15,612
Our next test replaced it with a small learned network containing about five hundred forty-one thousand adjustable numbers.

16
00:01:15,612 --> 00:01:20,449
The network reads a text embedding, which is a numerical summary of the sentence.

17
00:01:20,449 --> 00:01:25,107
It predicts the scene first, then the subject source, then the setting source.

18
00:01:25,107 --> 00:01:30,900
Only choosing the exact same source twice is forbidden; scene-consistent choices must be learned.

19
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:35,675
We hold back both a new paraphrase and specific subject-setting combinations.

20
00:01:35,675 --> 00:01:39,432
Held out means the learner does not see those examples during training.

21
00:01:39,432 --> 00:01:45,094
At evaluation, it must understand different wording and choose a source pair it did not memorize as a pair.

22
00:01:45,094 --> 00:01:48,480
Empty-text examples teach the network what no prompt looks like.

23
00:01:48,480 --> 00:01:51,920
This makes the test stricter than repeating the training phrases.

24
00:01:52,620 --> 00:01:56,828
We continued training and measured the held-out error every one hundred steps.

25
00:01:56,828 --> 00:01:59,255
The best result appeared at step one hundred.

26
00:01:59,255 --> 00:02:03,139
Ten later measurements, through step eleven hundred, did not improve it.

27
00:02:03,139 --> 00:02:07,455
That flat stretch is a plateau: more of the same training was no longer helping.

28
00:02:07,455 --> 00:02:13,820
The network separated correct wording from wrong wording, but it also began fitting the tiny training set too closely.

29
00:02:14,520 --> 00:02:20,669
The learned writer chose the correct scene for all nine held-out prompts and usually beat the causal controls.

30
00:02:20,669 --> 00:02:25,700
But only four of nine raw videos were matched to the right prompt, below our required six.

31
00:02:25,700 --> 00:02:27,154
So the learned gate fails.

32
00:02:27,154 --> 00:02:37,160
The useful conclusion is narrower: the network learned the tiny recipe syntax and responded to text, but the data and vocabulary were too small for robust prompt-to-video meaning.
